From: Vincenzo Grimaldi <vince.ceccarelli@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 19:00:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0046 a venue the book can reach today (5855 passed)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase D needed an API key and a wallet with BTC in it, and one of them had a
24-hour clock on it: testnet coins come from a web faucet — 1 BTC, once per
day, PC browser only, no API.

Bybit DEMO TRADING is a different module. api-demo.bybit.com, funded by
POST /v5/account/demo-apply-money (up to 15 BTC), running against REAL
mainnet market data with simulated matching. The marks, the basis and the
funding prints are the real ones; the fills are not and no real money moves.
So the wallet stops being a blocker and the market data gets BETTER than
testnet's, which has its own thin book and its own funding.

THE DANGER THAT CREATES. USE_TESTNET is a boolean that picks a base URL, and
adding a third destination to a boolean is how a testnet key authenticates
against mainnet.

  BYBIT_VENUE in {mainnet, testnet, demo} is the authority
  USE_TESTNET is DERIVED from it, so every existing gate keeps working and
    none of them has to learn a new word
  a config that states both and contradicts itself is REFUSED, not resolved

Two sources of truth about which exchange you are pointed at is precisely
the bug. Tests assert demo is a sandbox at every gate: is_live_authorized
False, assert_sandbox True, and the carry order gate returns (True, "DEMO")
— permitted for the same reason testnet is, and NAMED, so no log line and no
transcript can be read as mainnet evidence. One table maps venue to URL, so
there is exactly one answer to which exchange am I pointed at.

tools/fund_demo.py sends the funding request and checks the URL the client
is ACTUALLY pointed at rather than a config flag — that URL decides where
the request lands, and a funding call is a WRITE. Anything but the demo base
raises NotDemo and nothing is sent. It also refuses an amount above the
venue's own maximum and a coin it does not fund, so a typo is a message
rather than a rejected signed request nobody reads.

The drill now records the venue and what that venue's evidence is worth, and
defaults to "unknown" rather than to mainnet: a transcript that does not
know where it ran must not read as the strongest possible claim.

D30 — the drill died with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'numpy'`
from four imports deep under a bare interpreter. D16 again. The import moved
into _load_bot(); a failure names the virtualenv and exits 2, the same code
session_tail uses for "could not read" as distinct from "this is wrong".

scripts/deploy.sh is one command from a checkout to a running book:
flyctl, fly_stack --check BEFORE creating anything, app, volume, egress IP,
secrets, deploy --strategy immediate. Idempotent at every step. It REFUSES
MAINNET BY NAME — that needs a signed memo and a recorded drill first, and
then it is a deliberate act, not a script — and it ARMS NOTHING: a test
asserts every arming string appears only in the instructions it prints and
never in the part it executes, and that test was checked by breaking the
script on purpose and watching it fail.

What demo still does not give: F4 liquidation, F8 rate limits under load,
F10/F11 post-only fills, and nothing about slippage on a real book. It is
the fastest way to find out whether the SOFTWARE is right, which is a
different question from whether the trade is.

